LZSS installation
=================

Requirements
------------

Building from source requires GNU Make, GCC (or a compatible compiler
available as `gcc`), and the archiver `ar`. Installing also requires the
standard `install` utility. No external libraries are required.

The Makefile is intended for Linux and the Windows environments listed in
README.md, including MSYS2/MinGW and Cygwin.

Build
-----

From the repository directory, run:

    make

This builds the command-line application as `build/LZSS` (or
`build/LZSS.exe` under MinGW) and the static library as `build/libLZSS.a`.

The command-line application compresses or decompresses a file:

    build/LZSS e input-file compressed-file
    build/LZSS d compressed-file output-file

The operation may also be specified with uppercase `E` or `D`.

Library
-------

The library provides encoding only. Include `LibLZSS.h` and link with
`libLZSS.a`. `LZSSEncode` takes a source buffer, its size, the common
initialization byte, and a destination buffer; it returns the number of
encoded bytes written (or zero for empty input). The caller must provide a
destination buffer large enough for the encoded output.

For example, with the library installed in the default prefix:

    gcc -o my-app my-app.c -I/usr/local/include -L/usr/local/lib -lLZSS

Install
-------

Build the application and library, then install them:

    make
    make install

By default, files are installed under `/usr/local`: the executable goes in
`bin`, the library in `lib`, and `LibLZSS.h` in `include`. A pkg-config file
is also written to `lib/pkgconfig/LZSS.pc`.

To select another prefix, pass `PREFIX` to both commands, for example:

    make PREFIX="$HOME/.local"
    make install PREFIX="$HOME/.local"

Use `DESTDIR` with `make install` to stage files under a temporary root, for
example when creating a package:

    make install PREFIX=/usr DESTDIR=/tmp/package-root \
        PKGCONFIG_DIR=/tmp/package-root/usr/lib/pkgconfig

Uninstall files from the selected prefix with:

    make uninstall

Pass the same `PREFIX` used during installation when it was customized.
